course: dictionary
lesson: 14173
title: Moisturise in context
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: You do not need to know every word to understand a text. When you meet a new word, look at the words around it, because they are its context and often give clues. «Lily has dry, cracked hands in winter, so she moisturises them every night.» Dry and cracked are the clues: Lily puts something on her hands to stop them being dry, so moisturise must mean to make skin soft again.
explain: Context works for other things too, not just skin. «The baker added a little oil to moisturise the dough.» Dough is not skin, but the clue is oil: something is being added to stop it being dry. So moisturise can also mean to make something less dry by adding a little water or oil.
explain: A good check is to swap your meaning into the sentence. Try «Lily puts cream on her hands every night» in place of the hard word. If it still makes sense, your meaning is probably right. If it does not, read the sentence again or look the word up in a dictionary.
fact: «Moisturise» means to put cream or lotion on your skin so that it stays soft and does not get dry.
fact: «Moisturise» can also mean to make something less dry by adding a little water or oil to it.
fact: Skin can lose water, so it can become dry, which is why people moisturise it, especially in winter or after swimming.
fact: «Moisturising cream» is a cream that helps skin stay soft.
fact: In British English the word is spelled «moisturise»; in American English it is spelled «moisturize».
exercise: «Sam moisturises his dry hands every night.» What does moisturise mean here? | To cut his nails short | *To put cream on his skin so it stays soft | To hide his hands under a blanket | To wash his hands in hot water
exercise: «The baker added a little oil to moisturise the dough.» What does moisturise mean in this sentence? | To bake the dough until it is hard | To cut the dough into small pieces | To make the dough colder | *To make the dough less dry by adding a little oil
exercise: «Mum uses moisturising cream after swimming.» Find and copy the word that comes from moisturise. | after | swimming | cream | *moisturising
test: «After swimming, Mum moisturises her skin with lotion.» What is the lotion most likely to do for her skin? | Remove all the hair | Turn it blue | *Keep it soft | Make it dry and rough
test: Ella asks, «Is moisturise spelled the same in America?» What is the answer? | *No, Americans write moisturize, with a z, but the meaning is the same | No, Americans use a completely different word for it | Yes, it is always spelled moisturise | Yes, but it means to dry something out
exam: Which sentence uses moisturise correctly? | The cat will moisturise its tail before the race | The bus will moisturise the road | We will moisturise the football match tonight | *Dad wants to moisturise his cracked heels before bed
